        "kind": "ai-tool-descriptor",
        "id": "tool:your-files",
        "label": "Your files",
        "places_inputs": {
          "_what": "How a run is handed one of the person's files its input names (trillion-rust src/tool_runs/tools/input_files.rs reads this, and names no format, identity or call of its own): a row's input_schema field whose `format` is this `format` holds a file of the caller's by its content identity (one of `identity.prefixes`, then `identity.hex_digits` lowercase hex digits). Before the run's process starts, the engine asks `find.call` with the identity at `find.argument` for the file's `length_field`, `names_field` and `identity_field`, asks `bounds.call` for the most bytes one span answers (`span_field`), and reads the file in such spans with `read.call` {`name`: identity, `offset`, `length`, `raw`: true}, each answered as a file of its bytes (this row's session.byte_result), into the run's own folder, read-only, removed when the run ends; the file's local path stands where the identity was. Ported from the old harness's fileInputs and inputPlacement (a sealed container read a namespace file at /inputs/<hex>/<name>).",
          "format": "namespace-file",
          "identity": {
            "prefixes": [
              "1f:",
              "12:"
            ],
            "hex_digits": 64
          },
          "find": {
            "call": "find_file",
            "argument": "name_or_identity",
            "identity_field": "identity",
            "length_field": "byte_length",
            "names_field": "names"
          },
          "bounds": {
            "call": "bounds",
            "span_field": "span_most"
          },
          "read": {
            "call": "read_file_span",
            "name": "name_or_identity",
            "offset": "offset",
            "length": "length",
            "raw": "raw"
          }
        },
        "keeps_outputs": {
          "_what": "How the files a run makes are kept in the person's own files (trillion-rust src/tool_runs/tools/output_files.rs reads this, and names no word, call or field of its own): a row's `invoke.outputs` item whose `keep_in` is this `keep_in` is written here after the run's process exits 0, as the run's caller. The engine asks `bounds.call` for the most bytes one piece carries (`piece_field`), starts one upload with `start.call` {`upload_id`: 32 hex from the run's key, the output and the name, `name`: under `under`, then the row's label, then the name the input asks for or the run's start time, `length`, `media_type`, `app`: the row's id}, sends the file from the start's `next_field` on in pieces with `piece.call` {`upload_id`, `name`, `offset`}, each piece's bytes sent raw (this row's session.byte_argument), finishes with `finish.call` and keeps the output's results with `results.call`; the run's answer holds files.<output> = {identity, name, bytes, media_type}. A start asked again answers the same upload (open, or finished with its identity), so nothing is written twice. Ported from the old harness's keepOutput, keptName and keptResults (tools/shared-code/src/repo-explorer.mjs).",
          "keep_in": "files",
          "under": "/Made by tools",
          "bounds": {
            "call": "bounds",
            "piece_field": "piece_most"
          },
          "start": {
            "call": "write_file_start",
            "upload_id": "upload_id",
            "name": "name",
            "length": "byte_length",
            "media_type": "media_type",
            "app": "app",
            "state_field": "state",
            "finished": "finished",
            "next_field": "next_offset",
            "identity_field": "identity"
          },
          "piece": {
            "call": "write_file_piece",
            "upload_id": "upload_id",
            "name": "name",
            "offset": "offset",
            "next_field": "next_offset"
          },
          "finish": {
            "call": "write_file_finish",
            "upload_id": "upload_id",
            "name": "name",
            "identity_field": "identity"
          },
          "results": {
            "call": "record_file_results",
            "results": "results",
            "identity": "identity",
            "kind": "kind",
            "value": "value",
            "produced_by": "produced_by"
          }
        },
        "offers_runs": {
          "_what": "What a run of another tool may be granted of these calls (trillion-rust src/account_rights/run_capability/call-rights.json): a row asks for it in its stored list as {files: <folder>, operations: [<call>]}, \"/\" for every name or a folder such as \"/sites/\", and gets exactly those calls, each touching only the names under that folder. Each rule says which argument of a call names a name (`id`, each of them for a move or a copy) or a folder (`id_prefix`); a call by a file's identity, or one that names no name (`{}`), is covered only by \"/\". A call not listed here is never granted to a run. share_file is granted so a run may hand out a time-bound link to a name under its folder (tool:read-published-clips links the clips under /published-clips/), never a name outside it.",
          "right": "files",
          "operations": {
            "list_files": {
              "id_prefix": "folder"
            },
            "find_file": {
              "id": "name_or_identity"
            },
            "show_file": {
              "id": "name_or_identity"
            },
            "read_file": {
              "id": "name_or_identity"
            },
            "read_file_span": {
              "id": "name_or_identity"
            },
            "share_file": {
              "id": "name_or_identity"
            },
            "write_file": {
              "id": "name"
            },
            "write_file_start": {
              "id": "name"
            },
            "write_file_piece": {
              "id": "name"
            },
            "write_file_finish": {
              "id": "name"
            },
            "write_file_cancel": {
              "id": "name"
            },
            "find_names": {
              "id_prefix": "prefix"
            },
            "move_names": {
              "id": [
                "from",
                "to"
              ]
            },
            "copy_names": {
              "id": [
                "from",
                "to"
              ]
            },
            "set_aside": {
              "id": "name"
            },
            "put_back": {
              "id": "name"
            },
            "list_set_aside": {
              "id_prefix": "folder"
            },
            "make_folder": {
              "id": "name"
            },
            "bounds": {}
          }
        },
        "job": "Every name of the person's files, listed, followed as it changes, read by range, written, moved, shared and released, for an AI or a device's folder, through the engine's one door",
        "description": "The person's files (tools/live-namespace) as a session the engine keeps open and reaches only by calls: tools:run at @tools {tool: \"tool:your-files\", call: {name, arguments}} sends one MCP tools/call to the account's one session of this row, which the first call starts (trillion-rust src/tool_runs/tools/sessions.rs `call`); each call is answered on its own, so a list_changes that waits holds back no read. `name` is an operation of mcp-tools.json: list_files, list_changes, show_item, read_file_span, write_file, write_file_start, write_file_piece, write_file_finish, move_names, set_aside, put_back, make_folder, set_pin_rule, thumbnail, bounds, this_computer and the rest. The answer is {tool, result} with the operation's answer in result.structuredContent; a refusal is result.isError with {code, outcome}. read_file_span with raw: true answers the span's bytes themselves: {byte_slice: {total, offset, length, base64}}, or, to a request that accepts application/octet-stream, the raw bytes as the HTTP body with X-Total-Bytes and X-Offset (session.byte_result), so they cross the wire once, the first of them as soon as the store sends it. write_file_piece takes its piece raw the same way: a POST whose Content-Type is application/octet-stream, whose body is the piece and whose X-Call header is the base64url of the tools/call JSON; the engine names the body it kept as the call's content_file (session.byte_argument). The run opens no port. Its bytes are the person's own files-store connection, each field mounted on the descriptor settings.json `attached` names and never printed. Its names are rows a session of tool:your-files-names keeps (the one run that holds the person's database login), reached by calls through this run's door (invoke.records, descriptor 9): the engine lets one process reach only hosts every value it holds may go to, so a run holding the byte store's key and the database login could reach none. The program is the live-namespace this computer keeps among its program folders.",
